{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 5,
  "estimated_minutes": 30,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish Brandeis’s disciplinary findings against John from the court’s motion-to-dismiss review, and understand why substantive rules from earlier handbooks governed the alleged conduct."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The relevant background is Massachusetts private-university 'basic fairness' review and the Special Examiner’s use of the federal Dear Colleague Letter’s definition of sexual violence, rather than constitutional due process directly."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the violence issue requires reading § 2.1.d alongside the imported sexual-violence definition and the court’s discussion connecting consent, incapacity, and the wake-up kisses."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The court plainly explains its concerns about relationship context and ordinary meanings of violence, but ultimately treats substantive shortcomings as reinforcement for procedural unfairness rather than independently deciding substantive liability."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Although the wake-up kisses connect both defects, d01 concerns relationship context in assessing consent under § 3.3, while d02 concerns labeling unwanted contact as violence under § 2.1.d."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02 — The reviewer must distinguish § 2.1.d’s prohibition of unwanted physical contact from the Special Examiner’s imported definition of sexual violence and the court’s ordinary-meaning criticism. The cited passage describes and questions the Examiner’s reasoning without definitively resolving the full scope of the prohibition.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable distinguishing contract construction from criticism of an adjudicator’s application of a policy. The consent issue is relatively accessible: a relationship does not itself establish consent, but can inform whether conduct communicated consent. The violence issue needs closer reading because the prohibition, its examples, and an external definition operate at different levels. The reviewer should read the substantive-fairness conclusion as well as the cited passages to avoid treating the court’s concerns as a final determination that the conduct was consensual or outside every handbook prohibition."
}
